dict(img=None,
stem="A 46-year-old woman, gravida 3, para 3, comes to the office to establish care. Her history includes an appendectomy at age 20, lactose intolerance, preeclampsia that required delivery at 35 weeks' gestation in two of her pregnancies, and breastfeeding each child for 12 months. She uses a copper intrauterine device. Menarche occurred at age 13. She does not smoke. Her blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg, and BMI is 24 kg/m2. Fasting lipid and glucose concentrations are within the reference ranges.{FIG}Which of the following factors in her history most increases her risk of future cardiovascular disease?",
choices=["Age at menarche","Appendectomy","Breastfeeding history","Copper intrauterine device","Preeclampsia in two pregnancies"], key=4,
exp="Slide 11's <b>female-specific risk enhancers</b>: age at menarche, postmenopausal status, and <b>pregnancy-related complications</b>. <b>Preeclampsia</b>, a placentally mediated syndrome of hypertension and proteinuria (2–8% of pregnancies), predicts later heart failure, CHD, stroke and chronic hypertension. Recurrence and preterm delivery strengthen the signal. Her menarche at <b>13</b> is the nadir of the U-shaped risk curve. Her normal BP and lipids are the decoy: today's numbers don't erase the pregnancy signal.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Preeclampsia is a female-specific risk enhancer for later cardiovascular disease, even when current risk factors are normal.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct if menarche had occurred at 10 or younger (RR 1.27) or at 17 or older.",
1:"Would be correct for no cardiovascular outcome. Remote abdominal surgery isn't a CV risk factor.",
2:"Would be correct for no lecture risk. If anything, breastfeeding is associated with lower maternal CV risk.",
3:"Would be correct for no cardiovascular risk. Copper IUDs are hormone-free (unlike estrogen-containing contraception)."},
eli5="Pregnancy is like a stress test for the blood vessels. Failing it twice with preeclampsia is a warning that heart trouble is more likely later."),

dict(img='Wmenarche', cap='Relative risk of incident coronary heart disease by age at menarche in 1.2 million women',
stem="Investigators followed 1.2 million women (mean age 56 years) without known cardiovascular disease at baseline and recorded incident coronary heart disease by self-reported age at menarche. Results are shown, with the reference category at age 13.{FIG}Which of the following conclusions is best supported by these data?",
choices=["U-shaped: highest at ≤ 10 years, lowest at 13","Risk falls steadily with each later year of menarche","Risk is unrelated to age at menarche","Risk rises steadily with each later year of menarche","Risk rises only when menarche occurs at 17 years or older"], key=0,
exp="Canoy (Circulation 2015): the most common menarche age was 13 (25%), which is the lowest-risk reference. <b>Menarche at ≤ 10 → RR 1.27</b> (1.22–1.31). Risk falls toward 13, then <b>rises again with later menarche (≥ 17, RR 1.23)</b>. The relationship is <b>U-shaped</b>, so linear descriptions fail.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Age at menarche has a U-shaped relationship with CHD: highest at ≤ 10 years, lowest at 13, rising again with late menarche.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct if the ≥ 17 category had the lowest risk.",
2:"Would be correct if all confidence intervals crossed 1.0. The extremes clearly don't.",
3:"Would be correct if the ≤ 10 category had the lowest risk.",
4:"Would be correct if the ≤ 10 and 11 categories sat at 1.0."},
eli5="Girls whose periods start very early or very late both have more heart disease later. Around 13 is the sweet spot."),

