dict(img=None,
stem="A 29-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 0, at 35 weeks' gestation comes to the hospital because of a 1-day history of headache. Her blood pressure is 158/104 mm Hg on two readings 4 hours apart; at her first prenatal visit it was 112/70 mm Hg. Urine protein:creatinine ratio is 0.6. Ultrasonography shows fetal growth below the 10th percentile. She is delivered by induction of labor, and her blood pressure normalizes by 6 weeks postpartum.{FIG}Over the next 20 years, this patient is at greatest increased risk for which of the following?",
choices=["Breast cancer","Chronic hypertension","Hypothyroidism","Osteoporosis","Type 1 diabetes mellitus"], key=1,
exp="New hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria and fetal growth restriction → <b>preeclampsia</b>, a multisystem, placentally mediated disease (usually after 32 weeks, 2–8% of pregnancies). A history of preeclampsia <b>raises future cardiovascular risk</b> (Ahmed, JACC 2014). Board add-on: about <b>4-fold chronic hypertension</b> and about double CHD, stroke and heart failure. Normal postpartum BP is the decoy: the long-term risk persists. In the year after delivery, preeclampsia predicted heart failure (OR ~4.1), CHD and stroke (Savitz).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Preeclampsia strongly predicts later chronic hypertension and cardiovascular disease.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for nulliparity or late first pregnancy, not preeclampsia.",
2:"Would be correct for postpartum thyroiditis or autoimmune history.",
3:"Would be correct for long-term glucocorticoids, early menopause or low weight.",
4:"Would be correct only for autoimmune diabetes. Gestational diabetes predicts type 2."},
eli5="Preeclampsia is the blood vessels failing the pregnancy stress test, and high blood pressure often comes back for good later."),

